\label{remark3}
 Since $C_Z^\perp$ is also a subset of a supercode $C'_X\triangleq \ker (H'_X)$ of $C_X$, where $H'_X$ is a $J'\times L$ polynomial submatrix of $H_X$ obtained by taking a smaller set of rows, then the code $\mathcal  Q'(C'_X, C_Z) $ can be constructed with a larger dimension, and possibly the same minimum distance, as the next example shows. 

The second remark is that the generalized bicycle code \cite{kovalev2013quantum} given by $$H_X=\begin{bmatrix} a(x)& b(x) \end{bmatrix}, H_Z=\begin{bmatrix} b^\mathsf{T}(x)& a^\mathsf{T}(x) \end{bmatrix}$$ is an example of a code obtained by Theorem~\ref{minors-code}.

We take $N$ variable, and
\begin{align*}&H(x)=  \begin{bmatrix}   1+x^2 &1+x^4& 1+x^6& 1+x^8\\
                            x^4+x^{12}& x^{20} +x^{22}& x^{30}+ x^{42}& x^{40} +x^{14}\\
                             1+x^4 &x^{30}+x^{24}& x^{12}+x^{14}& x^3+x^{13} \end{bmatrix}. \end{align*}     
We compute
\begin{align*}
&\gamma_1=x^2+1, \quad \gamma_2=(x^2+1)^2, \quad \gamma_3=(x^2+1)^3, \\ 
&\frac{\gamma_1}{\gamma_0}=\frac{\gamma_2}{\gamma_1}=\frac{\gamma_3}{\gamma_2}= x^2+1 \Longrightarrow \text{ for all } 1\leq i\leq 3,  \\
&d_i=\gcd(x^2+1, x^N+1)=\left\{\begin{matrix} x^2+1, & \text{ if }  N \text{ even} \\
 x+1,& \text{ if }  N \text{ odd}
\end{matrix}\right.. \end{align*}

\label{prop:orthogonality}
The codes above satisfy $C_Z^\perp\subseteq C_X$. Hence $\mathcal Q = \mathcal Q 
(C_X,C_Z)$ is a quantum code of length $3N$.

\label{prop:residues}
Let $p\mid x^N+1$ be irreducible, and suppose that not all of $a,b,c$ vanish modulo $p$. The three minors of~\eqref{eq:HXshift} vanish modulo $p$ if and only if there are nonzero $s,\lambda\in\mathbb F_2[x]/(p)$ such that
\begin{equation}
 (a,b,c)=s(1,\lambda^{-1},\lambda^{-2}),\qquad \lambda^3=x^t
 \label{eq:cubic}
\end{equation}
in that field. In particular, for $t=0$ this becomes
\begin{equation}
 (a,b,c)=s(1,\lambda^2,\lambda),\qquad \lambda^3=1.
 \label{eq:unity}
\end{equation}

Both rows of~\eqref{eq:HXshift} are nonzero. Their minors vanish in the field $\mathbb F_2[x]/(p)$ precisely when the second row is $\lambda$ times the first. Thus
\begin{equation}
 x^tc=\lambda a,\qquad a=\lambda b,\qquad b=\lambda c.
\end{equation}
All three entries and $\lambda$ are nonzero in $\mathbb F_2[x]/(p)$. Setting $s=a$ yields~\eqref{eq:cubic}. Conversely,~\eqref{eq:cubic} makes the rows proportional.

\label{prop}
The row spaces of the circulant expansions of $z$ and $qz$ are equal if and only if
\begin{equation}
\gcd\left(q,\frac{x^N+1}{g_1}\right)=1.
\label{eq:q}
\end{equation}
